{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Twenty-Nine.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.923,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Sugar Bowl.",
   "start": 2.5229999999999997,
   "end": 3.611,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes Bell has served tea in Tidewhistle Cove for ten years, and she knows how every single person in the village takes it. She never needs to ask.",
   "start": 5.011,
   "end": 14.142,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Saturday afternoon, the vicarage opened its gardens to visitors. In the vicar's study, on a little stand by the window, lay the church's oldest treasure, a tiny hand written book of sea shanties more than two hundred years old.",
   "start": 14.992,
   "end": 29.52,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The vicar's housekeeper had taken a tray of tea into the empty study at two o'clock, to have it ready for the vicar. On the tray was a pot of tea, a jug of milk, a cup, and a sugar bowl. She always fills the sugar bowl with exactly twelve lumps, she said, no more and no fewer, because the vicar is trying to cut down.",
   "start": 30.37,
   "end": 50.509,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "At three o'clock, the shanty book was gone. The pot had been used. The cup was half full of dark tea with no milk in it at all, and the milk jug was still full. In the sugar bowl, only nine lumps were left.",
   "start": 51.359,
   "end": 64.351,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The vicar takes his tea with milk and no sugar, and he had been out in the garden all afternoon. The housekeeper never drinks tea at all, only cocoa.",
   "start": 65.201,
   "end": 74.225,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three visitors had been seen going in and out of the vicarage that afternoon.",
   "start": 75.075,
   "end": 79.448,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Captain Quill?\" said Agnes, when the vicar listed them. \"He takes it strong, with plenty of milk, and no sugar. Loveday Nance takes hers with milk and one sugar. And Mr Bolitho, the gentleman staying at the inn, takes his black, with three sugars. He's had tea at my place every day this week.\"",
   "start": 80.298,
   "end": 98.367,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who took the book of sea shanties?",
   "start": 99.467,
   "end": 102.283,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 103.483,
   "end": 104.507,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 105.407,
   "end": 110.634,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 115.134,
   "end": 116.158,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Bolitho took the book.",
   "start": 117.258,
   "end": 119.007,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Whoever sat in the study and helped themselves to tea left a cup of dark tea with no milk, and the milk jug was untouched. The sugar bowl started with exactly twelve lumps and was down to nine, so three lumps had gone. Someone drank tea black, with three sugars.",
   "start": 119.857,
   "end": 136.199,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill takes his with plenty of milk and no sugar, and Loveday with milk and one sugar. The vicar takes milk and no sugar, and the housekeeper doesn't drink tea at all. Only Mr Bolitho takes his tea black, with three sugars.",
   "start": 137.049,
   "end": 151.619,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "When Agnes put this to him over the counter of the Kettle and Gull, Mr Bolitho sighed into his cup. He was a singer of sea shanties, he said, and had only meant to copy out a song or two, but he could not resist taking the book. He returned it to the vicar that evening, and on Sunday he sang three shanties from it in church, beautifully, to make up for it.",
   "start": 152.469,
   "end": 173.888,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
